    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i'm looking at some paperwork for a house i'm buying in seattle and it keeps mentioning \"escrow.\" i don't really get what that actually means or what the person in charge of it actually does. like why can't i just pay the seller directly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow is a process in real estate transactions where a third-party company holds money and documents until certain conditions are met, such as the completion of a sale. This ensures a smooth transfer of funds and ownership, protecting both the buyer and seller from potential disputes. The escrow officer acts as a neutral intermediary, managing the transfer of funds and verifying the execution of the sale. Since escrow involvement can have tax implications and dependence on property taxes being up to date, paying the seller directly could create complications with tax obligations.",
